course: dictionary
lesson: 61
title: Achieve: reaching a goal
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To achieve something means to succeed in doing something good, usually after effort. You can achieve a goal, a result, a target or a dream. Achieve is a verb, so it changes: achieves, achieved, achieving.
explain: Look at the clues around it. «A loophole is a gap in a rule that lets someone follow its words but break its spirit, what it is trying to achieve.» A rule is made for a purpose, so achieve here means reaching that purpose. Swap in reach and the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Achieve often goes with words like goal, success or dreams. "She achieved her dream" tells you she got what she wanted after hard work. Use achieve when the effort led to a good result.
fact: Achieve means to succeed in doing something good, usually after effort.
fact: Achieve also means to reach a goal, result or level.
fact: Useful phrases: achieve a goal, achieve success, achieve your dreams.
fact: «Achieve» is a verb: achieves, achieved, achieving.
fact: At school you can achieve a grade or a target, and you may receive an achievement award.
exercise: «A loophole is a gap in a rule that lets someone follow its words but break its spirit, what it is trying to achieve.» In this sentence, what does achieve mean? | Forget all about | *Reach the aim or goal | Break or cut into pieces | Sing loudly
exercise: Maya worked hard all term and achieved her reading target. What did she do? | Lent her book to a friend | Got lost in the library | Gave up on reading | *Reached her target after effort
exercise: Which phrase uses achieve correctly? | *Achieve a goal | Achieve the lunch bell | Achieve a cold | Achieve a sneeze
test:
Mr Patel said, «You can achieve great things if you keep trying.» What does achieve mean here? | Give up | *Succeed in doing something good | Lose your way | Forget the task
Last year the class ___ its reading goal. Which word fills the gap? | achieve | *achieved | achieves | achieving
exam:
Which sentence uses achieve correctly? | *She achieved her goal of reading fifty books. | The door achieved shut at noon. | I achieve my dinner every night. | The dog achieved a loud, sunny day.
